window.BIOLOGY_DATA={"objectives":[{"key":"microscopy-origins","title":"Early microscopy","pages":[3],"summary":"Microscopes made previously invisible living structures available for study.","bullets":["The lecture places the early development of European microscopy around 1590 AD.","Early observers examined materials including pond water and tree bark.","The slide illustrates microscopes: instruments extend the range of structures that scientists can observe."],"questions":[{"suffix":"evidence","stem":"Why was microscopy important to the development of cell biology?","options":["It proved that all organisms are multicellular.","It let scientists observe small structures in samples such as pond water and bark.","It replaced the need for observation with guesswork.","It made every cell visible to the unaided eye."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Microscopes allowed scientists to observe structures too small to resolve unaided, providing evidence for later ideas about cells."}]},{"key":"leeuwenhoek","title":"Antonie van Leeuwenhoek","pages":[4],"summary":"Leeuwenhoek used powerful microscopes to observe microorganisms.","bullets":["Antonie van Leeuwenhoek lived from 1632 to 1723.","The source describes him as a self-taught amateur naturalist.","He is associated with early observations of bacteria and protists using microscopes he made."],"questions":[{"suffix":"contribution","stem":"Which contribution is associated with Leeuwenhoek in this lecture?","options":["Concluding that all plant tissues consist of cells.","Naming cork compartments after publishing Micrographia.","Observing bacteria and protists with microscopes.","Describing protein pumps in the plasma membrane."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Leeuwenhoek is associated with observations of microorganisms, including bacteria and protists. His microscopes were not the first microscopes ever made."}],"clarification":"The slide credits him with the first microscope. Earlier microscopes existed; his important contribution was making effective instruments and observing microorganisms.","sources":[{"title":"Science Museum Group: Antoni van Leeuwenhoek","url":"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/people/cp37514/antoni-van-leeuwenhoek"},{"title":"OpenStax: Studying Cells","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells"}]},{"key":"hooke","title":"Robert Hooke and cork cells","pages":[5],"summary":"Hooke named the tiny compartments he saw in cork.","bullets":["Robert Hooke lived from 1635 to 1703.","He published Micrographia in 1665.","He examined thin slices of cork and saw box-like compartments.","The lecture gives his term as cellulae, connected with the modern word cell."],"questions":[{"suffix":"cork","stem":"Which observation is linked with Robert Hooke and Micrographia (1665)?","options":["Box-like compartments in thin cork slices.","ATP-dependent protein pumps.","The first chloroplast grana.","Water moving into a hypotonic cell."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hooke examined cork and described the small compartments that gave cells their name. These observations preceded the modern cell theory."}]},{"key":"schleiden","title":"Matthias Schleiden and plants","pages":[6],"summary":"Schleiden connected plant structure with cells.","bullets":["Matthias Schleiden lived from 1804 to 1881.","The lecture describes him as a lawyer and botanist.","He concluded that plants are composed of cells."],"questions":[{"suffix":"plants","stem":"Which conclusion is associated with Schleiden?","options":["Only animals contain cells.","All cells contain chloroplasts.","Cells can form without existing cells.","Plants are composed of cells."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Schleiden connected the study of plants with the idea that living structures are composed of cells."}],"clarification":"The slide spells his first name Mattias; the conventional spelling is Matthias.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Studying Cells","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells"}]},{"key":"schwann","title":"Theodor Schwann and animals","pages":[7],"summary":"Schwann connected animal tissues with cells.","bullets":["Theodor Schwann lived from 1810 to 1882.","The lecture describes him as a physician and physiologist.","He concluded that animal tissues are composed of cells."],"questions":[{"suffix":"animals","stem":"Which contribution is associated with Theodor Schwann?","options":["Naming the protein ATP.","Concluding that animal tissues are composed of cells.","Describing the three common bacterial shapes.","Calculating cube surface area."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Schwann extended the cellular view of living structures to animals; Schleiden is associated with the plant conclusion."}]},{"key":"virchow","title":"Rudolf Virchow and existing cells","pages":[8],"summary":"Virchow is associated with the principle that cells come from existing cells.","bullets":["Rudolf Virchow lived from 1821 to 1902.","The lecture associates him with the proposal that cells arise from pre-existing cells.","This principle becomes one of the three tenets of cell theory on the next slide."],"questions":[{"suffix":"origin","stem":"Which statement is associated with Virchow in the lecture?","options":["A cell must have a cellulose wall.","A cell can arise without a pre-existing cell.","Cells arise from pre-existing cells.","Only plants consist of cells."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The principle links new cells with existing cells, rather than treating cells as appearing spontaneously."}]},{"key":"cell-theory","title":"Cell theory","pages":[9],"summary":"Cell theory links living organisms, basic biological structure, and the origin of cells.","bullets":["Living organisms consist of one or more cells.","The cell is the basic unit of structure in organisms.","Cells arise from pre-existing cells."],"questions":[{"suffix":"tenets","stem":"Which set matches the three tenets of cell theory?","options":["Organisms consist of cells; cells are basic structural units; cells arise from existing cells.","Only plants have cells; tissues are the smallest units; new cells appear spontaneously.","All organisms have many cells; every cell has a wall; cells cannot divide.","Cells consist of organs; organs form DNA; organisms have no cellular structure."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cell theory applies to organisms with one cell or many cells and connects new cells with existing cells."}],"relatedCellKey":"theory"},{"key":"cell-categories","title":"Cells and the two broad categories","pages":[1,2,10],"summary":"Cells are living units, classified broadly as prokaryotic or eukaryotic.","bullets":["The lecture is titled Cell Biology.","Its core concepts are cell theory, what a cell is, prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells, organelles, membranes, and transport into and out of cells.","The source describes the cell as the simplest unit of living matter.","The two broad cell categories are prokaryotic and eukaryotic."],"questions":[{"suffix":"categories","stem":"Which pair gives the two broad categories of cells?","options":["Plant and tissue.","Nucleus and ribosome.","Organ and organism.","Prokaryotic and eukaryotic."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Prokaryotic and eukaryotic describe two broad forms of cellular organization. Plant and animal cells are both eukaryotic."}]},{"key":"prokaryotic-cells","title":"Prokaryotic cells","pages":[11,12,13],"summary":"Typical prokaryotic cells are relatively small and lack a membrane-bound nucleus.","bullets":["The source describes prokaryotic cells as small and structurally simple compared with typical eukaryotes.","They lack a membrane-bound nucleus and membrane-bound organelles in the introductory classroom model.","Their DNA is not enclosed in a nucleus; RNA also occurs in the cell.","Bacteria, including cyanobacteria, are examples. Page 13 shows bacterial colonies on a culture plate.","The word prokaryotic combines a prefix meaning before with a root referring to nucleus."],"questions":[{"suffix":"identify","stem":"A small bacterial cell contains DNA but no membrane-bound nucleus. Which description fits?","options":["Eukaryotic, because it contains DNA.","Prokaryotic, because its DNA is not enclosed in a nucleus.","A plant cell, because all bacteria are algae.","Nonliving, because it lacks a nucleus."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prokaryotic cells can carry out life processes without a membrane-bound nucleus. Cyanobacteria are bacteria, not eukaryotic algae."}],"clarification":"The slide calls cyanobacteria unicellular algae and explains kary as genes. Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic bacteria, and the root refers to nucleus. Not all cyanobacteria occur as isolated single cells.","relatedCellKey":"prokaryote","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Prokaryotic Cells","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells"},{"title":"OpenStax: Algae","url":"https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/5-4-algae"}]},{"key":"bacterial-shapes","title":"Common bacterial shapes","pages":[12,13],"summary":"The source distinguishes round, rod-shaped, and spiral bacterial forms.","bullets":["Cocci are round or spherical.","Bacilli are rod-shaped.","Spirillum is the singular term used for a spiral form; spirilla is plural.","Use the shape illustrations on page 12; the culture plate on page 13 shows colonies rather than the detailed shape of each individual cell."],"questions":[{"suffix":"shape","stem":"A bacterium is described as rod-shaped. Which term matches?","options":["Coccus.","Spirillum.","Bacillus.","Nucleolus."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bacillus is a rod-shaped bacterial form. Coccus refers to a round form and spirillum to a spiral form."}]},{"key":"eukaryotic-cells","title":"Eukaryotic cells","pages":[14,15],"summary":"Typical eukaryotic cells have a nucleus and compartmentalized internal structures.","bullets":["The prefix eu means true or real.","The lecture lists multicellular algae, protozoans, fungi, plants, and animals as eukaryotes.","Typical eukaryotic cells are larger and structurally more complex than typical prokaryotic cells.","They have membrane-bound organelles; the nucleus is an important distinguishing compartment."],"questions":[{"suffix":"classification","stem":"A unicellular protozoan has a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. How is its cell classified?","options":["Eukaryotic.","Prokaryotic because it is unicellular.","A bacterium because it is microscopic.","A virus because it has internal structures."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Eukaryotic classification depends on cellular organization, not on whether the whole organism is unicellular or multicellular."}],"clarification":"The slide list is not exhaustive: eukaryotes include unicellular algae as well. Descriptions of size and organelles refer to typical cells, not absolute rules for every specialized mature cell.","relatedCellKey":"eukaryote","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Eukaryotic Cells","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells"},{"title":"OpenStax: Algae","url":"https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/5-4-algae"}]},{"key":"organelles","title":"Organelles and compartmentalization","pages":[15],"summary":"Organelles are specialized structures that carry out particular cellular functions.","bullets":["The source defines organelles as specialized structures that perform specific functions.","Membrane-bound compartments allow different activities to occur in different areas of a eukaryotic cell.","Not every specialized cell structure has a surrounding membrane; ribosomes are a later example."],"questions":[{"suffix":"function","stem":"Why does the lecture describe organelles as specialized structures?","options":["Every organelle has the same task.","They are complete organisms living independently.","They are tissues composed of many cells.","Different structures perform particular functions inside the cell."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Specialization means a structure supports particular cellular activities. Ribosomes demonstrate that specialized structures need not all be membrane-bound."}],"relatedCellKey":"organelles"},{"key":"plant-animal-models","title":"Plant and animal cell models","pages":[16,17],"summary":"Identify the structures labeled in the source plant and animal diagrams.","bullets":["Plant diagram labels: cell wall, cell membrane, cytoplasm, chloroplast, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi body, mitochondria, nucleus, nucleolus, and vacuole.","Animal diagram labels: cell membrane, cytoplasm, Golgi body, nucleus, mitochondria, smooth ER, rough ER, and vacuole.","The plant model contains both chloroplasts and mitochondria.","Use multiple structures to distinguish typical models; having a nucleus alone does not distinguish plant from animal."],"questions":[{"suffix":"compare","stem":"Which structure combination best identifies the photosynthetic plant model in the source?","options":["Nucleus and cell membrane only.","Cell wall, chloroplasts, and a prominent vacuole.","Mitochondria and cytoplasm only.","Rough ER and Golgi body only."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Typical plant and animal cells share many structures. A cell wall and chloroplasts provide additional clues in the plant diagram."}],"clarification":"The lists show selected labels, not everything a plant or animal cell can contain. For example, omission of nucleolus from the animal list does not mean animal cells lack it.","relatedCellKey":"comparison","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Eukaryotic Cells","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells"}]},{"key":"cilia","title":"Cilia","pages":[18],"summary":"Cilia are usually short, numerous projections used for movement.","bullets":["Cilia can cover all or part of a cell.","They can propel a cell or move substances across its surface.","In the eukaryotic structures shown, protein microtubules contribute to their internal structure."],"questions":[{"suffix":"movement","stem":"Many short projections move material across a cell surface. Which structures best fit?","options":["Vacuoles.","Chromosomes.","Cilia.","Golgi cisternae."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cilia are usually numerous and short. They can move surrounding material or propel the cell itself."}],"relatedCellKey":"cilia"},{"key":"flagella","title":"Flagella","pages":[18],"summary":"Flagella are usually longer and fewer than cilia.","bullets":["Flagellum is singular; flagella is plural.","The source shows one or a few long projections used for locomotion.","Like cilia, eukaryotic flagella project from the cell and contain protein microtubules."],"questions":[{"suffix":"number-length","stem":"Which description best fits a eukaryotic flagellum in this lecture?","options":["A long projection that can propel a cell.","A large storage compartment inside the cell.","A stack of membranes that sorts proteins.","A short DNA strand inside the nucleus."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Flagella are typically long movement structures present in small numbers, in contrast with the many shorter cilia."}],"clarification":"The microtubule description concerns eukaryotic cilia and flagella. Bacterial flagella have a different structure.","relatedCellKey":"flagella","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Cytoskeleton","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-5-the-cytoskeleton"}]},{"key":"cell-wall","title":"Cell wall","pages":[19],"summary":"Cell walls provide external support and protection.","bullets":["The source emphasizes cell support and protection.","Plant cell walls contain cellulose, formed from linked beta-glucose units.","Fungal cell walls contain chitin.","The plant-tissue image shows boundaries surrounding adjacent cells."],"questions":[{"suffix":"materials","stem":"Which pairing is correct?","options":["Plant wall: chitin; fungal wall: cellulose.","Animal wall: cellulose; plant wall: protein only.","All cell walls: the same phospholipid bilayer as the membrane.","Plant wall: cellulose; fungal wall: chitin."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cell-wall composition differs among groups. The wall supports and protects a cell; it is distinct from the plasma membrane."}],"clarification":"The slide groups all algae with cellulose-walled plants. Algal coverings vary; cellulose is common in some algal groups but is not a universal algal-wall rule.","relatedCellKey":"wall","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Eukaryotic Cell Structures","url":"https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells"},{"title":"OpenStax: Algae","url":"https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/5-4-algae"}]},{"key":"plasma-membrane","title":"Plasma membrane","pages":[20],"summary":"The plasma membrane controls exchange across the cell boundary.","bullets":["The membrane selectively allows materials to enter and leave the cell.","The source calls it the outer living boundary.","It contains phospholipids and proteins.","When a cell wall is present, the plasma membrane lies inside the wall."],"questions":[{"suffix":"boundary","stem":"Which structure directly forms the selectively permeable boundary controlling entry and exit?","options":["Nucleolus.","Plasma membrane.","Chromatin.","Cilium."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The plasma membrane regulates exchange with the cell environment. A supporting cell wall does not replace this membrane."}],"relatedCellKey":"membrane"},{"key":"cytoplasm","title":"Cytoplasm","pages":[21],"summary":"Cytoplasm includes the cell interior outside the nucleus.","bullets":["In a eukaryotic cell, cytoplasm is everything inside the plasma membrane and outside the nucleus.","It includes fluid cytosol, the cytoskeleton, and the organelles outside the nucleus.","Water is a major component of the fluid.","The source uses cyto = cell and plasm = goop as an informal memory aid, not a technical definition."],"questions":[{"suffix":"location","stem":"Which description correctly locates cytoplasm in a eukaryotic cell?","options":["Only inside the nucleus.","Only outside the plasma membrane.","Inside the plasma membrane but outside the nucleus.","Only in the cell wall."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cytoplasm includes cytosol and structures outside the nucleus within the cell membrane. The fluid inside the nucleus is nucleoplasm."}],"relatedCellKey":"cytoplasm"},{"key":"cytoskeleton","title":"Cytoskeleton and support","pages":[21],"summary":"The cytoskeleton contributes to cell shape and internal support.","bullets":["The source lists the cytoskeleton as part of the cytoplasm.","Its protein-fiber framework helps support and organize the cell.","Cell shape depends on the cell framework and other structures, rather than on fluid alone."],"questions":[{"suffix":"support","stem":"Which part of the cytoplasm provides a protein-fiber framework for cell support?","options":["Cytoskeleton.","Nucleoplasm.","Cellulose alone.","Free DNA only."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The cytoskeleton is the internal protein framework that helps support and organize the cell."}],"relatedCellKey":"cytoskeleton"},{"key":"cytoplasmic-streaming","title":"Cytoplasmic streaming","pages":[21],"summary":"Movement within the cytoplasm helps distribute material.","bullets":["The source calls the movement of cytoplasmic fluid cytoplasmic streaming.","This movement helps disperse nutrients and other materials within the cell.","It is an internal movement process, not the definition of materials crossing a membrane."],"questions":[{"suffix":"identify","stem":"Nutrients are distributed by movement of fluid within a cell. Which source term describes this?","options":["Exocytosis.","Phagocytosis.","Cell division.","Cytoplasmic streaming."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cytoplasmic streaming refers to movement inside the cell that helps distribute material. It is different from transport across the cell boundary."}]},{"key":"nucleus","title":"Nucleus and hereditary material","pages":[22],"summary":"The nucleus encloses DNA and associated proteins within a double membrane.","bullets":["The source describes the nucleus as usually spherical or oval and often the largest visible internal structure.","It contains hereditary information as DNA.","A double membrane separates nuclear contents from cytoplasm.","The internal fluid is nucleoplasm.","DNA and proteins occur as chromatin or chromosomes.","The nucleus also contains nucleoli."],"questions":[{"suffix":"contents","stem":"Which combination correctly describes the nucleus?","options":["Cellulose wall, chlorophyll, and digestive enzymes.","A double membrane enclosing nucleoplasm and DNA with associated proteins.","No membrane, no DNA, and only water.","Protein channels surrounding a central vacuole."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The nucleus encloses nucleoplasm and hereditary DNA associated with proteins. Nucleoli are structures within it."}],"relatedCellKey":"nucleus"},{"key":"nucleolus","title":"Nucleolus and ribosome production","pages":[22],"summary":"Nucleoli inside the nucleus are involved in making ribosomal components.","bullets":["Nucleolus is singular; nucleoli is plural.","The source identifies nucleoli as sites of ribosome production.","More precisely, nucleoli produce rRNA and help assemble ribosomal subunits."],"questions":[{"suffix":"production","stem":"Which structure inside the nucleus is associated with ribosomal RNA and ribosomal-subunit assembly?","options":["Mitochondrion.","Lysosome.","Nucleolus.","Cell wall."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The nucleolus is involved in producing ribosomal components. It is not another name for the entire nucleus."}],"relatedCellKey":"nucleolus"},{"key":"rough-er","title":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","pages":[23],"summary":"Rough ER has surface ribosomes and supports protein handling.","bullets":["The ER is a membrane network enclosing internal spaces, described in the source as canals and narrow cavities.","It provides surfaces for chemical reactions and pathways for transport and handling of molecules.","Rough ER has ribosomes on its cytoplasmic surface.","Proteins are synthesized by ribosomes and enter associated processing and transport pathways.","ER membrane is continuous with the outer nuclear envelope."],"questions":[{"suffix":"roughness","stem":"What explains the rough appearance of rough ER?","options":["It is made of cellulose.","It contains a row of chloroplasts.","It is a completely rigid wall.","Ribosomes are attached to its surface."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Attached ribosomes give rough ER its rough appearance. Ribosomes synthesize proteins; the ER supports their processing and transport."}],"clarification":"The slide says ER is continuous with the cell membrane. ER is continuous with the outer nuclear envelope; transfer toward the Golgi and plasma membrane occurs through membrane traffic, rather than one uninterrupted ER-to-cell-surface membrane.","relatedCellKey":"rough-er","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Endomembrane System","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins"}]},{"key":"smooth-er","title":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","pages":[23],"summary":"Smooth ER lacks surface ribosomes and is associated with lipid synthesis.","bullets":["Smooth ER and rough ER are parts of the ER network.","Smooth ER lacks attached ribosomes.","Lipid synthesis is associated with smooth ER.","The source describes ER as providing reaction surfaces, internal transport paths, and temporary storage for synthesized molecules."],"questions":[{"suffix":"lipids","stem":"Which pairing is correct?","options":["Smooth ER: no attached ribosomes and lipid synthesis.","Smooth ER: many attached ribosomes and photosynthesis.","Rough ER: no ribosomes and a cellulose wall.","Smooth ER: DNA storage inside a nucleus."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The absence of attached ribosomes distinguishes smooth ER, which supports lipid synthesis."}],"relatedCellKey":"smooth-er"},{"key":"ribosomes","title":"Ribosomes","pages":[24],"summary":"Ribosomes are non-membrane-bound structures that synthesize proteins.","bullets":["The source describes ribosomes as small, dense bodies.","They are not membrane-bound.","They contain rRNA and proteins.","They may be free in cytoplasm, attached to rough ER, or organized as polysomes.","Their function is protein synthesis."],"questions":[{"suffix":"composition","stem":"Which statement correctly describes ribosomes?","options":["Membrane-bound sacs filled with chlorophyll.","Non-membrane-bound structures made of rRNA and proteins that synthesize proteins.","Cell walls made of beta-glucose.","Large fluid-filled compartments used only for water storage."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ribosomes contain both ribosomal RNA and proteins and carry out protein synthesis. They do not have a surrounding membrane."}],"clarification":"The source mentions strands of rRNA but leaves out ribosomal proteins. Both are components of a ribosome.","relatedCellKey":"ribosome","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Eukaryotic Cells","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells"}]},{"key":"polysomes","title":"Polysomes","pages":[24],"summary":"A polysome is a group of ribosomes translating the same messenger RNA.","bullets":["The source defines a polysome as a cluster of ribosomes.","The shared messenger RNA distinguishes a functional polysome from an arbitrary group of nearby ribosomes.","Multiple ribosomes can make protein from the same message at the same time."],"questions":[{"suffix":"cluster","stem":"Which arrangement is a polysome?","options":["One lysosome digesting several ribosomes.","Several nuclei sharing one cell wall.","Several ribosomes translating the same mRNA.","A stack of chloroplast thylakoids."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A polysome is several ribosomes translating one mRNA molecule, supporting protein production."}],"clarification":"The slide associates polysomes with proteins needed within the cell. Polysomes are not limited to that destination; free or ER-associated ribosomes may form them.","sources":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf: From RNA to Protein","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26829/"},{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf: The Endoplasmic Reticulum","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK26841/"}]},{"key":"golgi","title":"Golgi apparatus","pages":[25],"summary":"The Golgi processes and packages material into vesicles.","bullets":["The source describes stacks of flattened membrane sacs called cisternae.","It gives four to eight discs as a classroom example, not a universal count.","Edges can bud off membrane vesicles.","The Golgi handles and packages proteins, lipids, and carbohydrates for destinations including secretion.","Its relationship with ER involves transport traffic."],"questions":[{"suffix":"packaging","stem":"Which structure consists of stacked cisternae and packages material into budding vesicles?","options":["Cytoskeleton.","Nucleolus.","Cell wall.","Golgi apparatus."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Golgi receives and handles materials, then packages them for delivery, including secretion outside the cell."}],"clarification":"The slide says Golgi may connect to ER. Teach the transport-vesicle connection rather than a general claim of direct, continuous membrane between the two organelles.","relatedCellKey":"golgi","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Endomembrane System","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins"}]},{"key":"mitochondria","title":"Mitochondria and ATP","pages":[26],"summary":"Mitochondria use energy from respiration to support ATP production.","bullets":["The source shows mitochondria as bean-shaped or elongated structures distributed in cytoplasm.","A double membrane surrounds a fluid-filled matrix.","The outer membrane is relatively smooth; folds in the inner membrane increase its surface area.","Mitochondria are a major site of aerobic cellular respiration.","ATP stands for adenosine triphosphate and acts as an energy-transfer molecule in cells.","The source diagram summarizes respiration: glucose + oxygen produces carbon dioxide + water, with energy captured in ATP. In formulas: C6H12O6 + 6 O2 -> 6 CO2 + 6 H2O + energy (captured in ATP)."],"questions":[{"suffix":"energy","stem":"How do folded inner membranes support mitochondrial function?","options":["They replace the need for cellular respiration.","They provide surface area for reactions involved in ATP production.","They store all of the cell's hereditary DNA.","They convert the mitochondrion into a chloroplast."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inner-membrane folds increase area for respiration-related processes. Mitochondria contribute to ATP production using energy from food."},{"suffix":"respiration","stem":"Which inputs and outputs match the respiration summary on page 26?","options":["Carbon dioxide + water becomes glucose + oxygen without energy input.","Cellulose + chitin becomes a protein pump.","Glucose + oxygen yields carbon dioxide + water, with energy transferred to ATP.","ATP is made directly from sunlight in a mitochondrion."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The source equation summarizes aerobic respiration: C6H12O6 + 6 O2 -> 6 CO2 + 6 H2O, releasing energy that supports ATP production."}],"relatedCellKey":"mitochondria"},{"key":"vacuoles","title":"Vacuoles","pages":[27],"summary":"Vacuoles are membrane-enclosed compartments used for storage.","bullets":["The source groups vacuoles, vesicles, and lysosomes as membrane-enclosed compartments.","Vacuoles are described as relatively large storage compartments.","The image shows a large vacuole in a plant-cell model.","Shape and size are schematic; not every compartment is literally a sphere."],"questions":[{"suffix":"storage","stem":"Which term does the source use for a relatively large membrane-enclosed storage compartment?","options":["Vacuole.","Ribosome.","Cilium.","Nucleolus."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Vacuoles store material within a surrounding membrane and are often especially prominent in mature plant-cell models."}],"relatedCellKey":"vacuole"},{"key":"vesicles","title":"Vesicles","pages":[25,27],"summary":"Vesicles are small membrane-enclosed transport or storage compartments.","bullets":["The source contrasts tiny vesicles with larger vacuoles.","Vesicles can carry material between compartments or toward the cell surface.","Page 25 connects vesicle formation with budding from Golgi membranes."],"questions":[{"suffix":"carrier","stem":"Which description best fits a vesicle?","options":["A naked RNA strand inside a nucleus.","A cellulose tube outside a plant cell.","A permanent external movement projection.","A small membrane-enclosed compartment that can carry material."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Vesicles transport or store material in membrane-enclosed packages. Their movement is part of cellular membrane traffic."}],"relatedCellKey":"vesicle"},{"key":"lysosomes","title":"Lysosomes","pages":[27],"summary":"Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes in membrane-enclosed compartments.","bullets":["The source calls lysosomes specialized compartments.","They contain digestive enzymes, including proteolytic enzymes that break down proteins.","Digestive contents are enclosed by a membrane."],"questions":[{"suffix":"enzymes","stem":"Which feature distinguishes a lysosome in the source?","options":["Cellulose fibers in a rigid external wall.","Digestive enzymes inside a membrane-enclosed compartment.","A stack of light-capturing thylakoids.","A cluster of ribosomes sharing one mRNA."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lysosomes contain enzymes for breaking down material. Proteolytic refers specifically to protein breakdown."}],"relatedCellKey":"lysosome"},{"key":"chloroplasts","title":"Chloroplasts and thylakoids","pages":[28],"summary":"Chloroplasts contain structures and molecules required for photosynthesis.","bullets":["Chloroplasts occur in photosynthetic cells of plants and algae.","The diagram labels an outer membrane, inner membrane, and internal thylakoid.","Chlorophyll is a light-absorbing pigment associated with thylakoid membranes.","A thylakoid is an internal membrane sac; a stack is a granum, and grana is plural.","Photosynthesis includes processes in thylakoid membranes and in the surrounding stroma."],"questions":[{"suffix":"stack","stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes thylakoids and grana?","options":["A granum is the outer chloroplast membrane.","A thylakoid is a whole mitochondrion.","Thylakoids are membrane sacs; stacks of them are grana.","Grana are ribosomes producing chlorophyll."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The source wording calls stacks thylakoids. More precisely, individual sacs are thylakoids and stacks are grana."}],"clarification":"The slide calls the stacks thylakoids and puts all photosynthesis enzymes there. Distinguish individual thylakoids, grana stacks, and stroma; not all photosynthetic reactions occur in the thylakoid membrane.","relatedCellKey":"chloroplast","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Overview of Photosynthesis","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis"}]},{"key":"fluid-mosaic","title":"Phospholipid bilayer and fluid mosaic","pages":[29,30,31],"summary":"A dynamic phospholipid bilayer contains a mixture of membrane components.","bullets":["Phospholipids form two layers with proteins distributed through the membrane.","Each phospholipid has a hydrophilic phosphate-containing head and hydrophobic lipid tails.","Hydro refers to water; philic means attracted to water, and phobic means avoiding water.","In water, hydrophobic tails face inward toward each other, while hydrophilic heads face the watery environments on both sides.","The source diagrams also show membrane proteins and cholesterol.","Fluid means that many components can move laterally within the membrane.","Mosaic refers to the mixture of different components."],"questions":[{"suffix":"orientation","stem":"How do phospholipids orient in a bilayer exposed to water on both sides?","options":["Hydrophobic tails face the water and heads face each other.","Hydrophilic heads face water; hydrophobic tails face inward toward one another.","All parts face outward in a single layer.","Proteins replace all phospholipids."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hydrophilic heads interact with water, while hydrophobic tails are shielded in the bilayer interior."},{"suffix":"model","stem":"A membrane protein moves sideways within a bilayer containing different components. Which model does this illustrate?","options":["A rigid cellulose wall.","A solid protein crystal.","The fluid-mosaic model.","The spontaneous formation of cells."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lateral movement illustrates fluidity, and the mixed components illustrate the mosaic. The membrane is dynamic rather than a fixed rigid shell."}],"clarification":"Cholesterol is prominent in animal plasma-membrane models; do not assume every membrane in every organism has the same lipid composition.","relatedCellKey":"bilayer","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Membrane Components and Structure","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-1-components-and-structure"}]},{"key":"selective-permeability","title":"Selective permeability and molecule properties","pages":[32],"summary":"Membrane permeability depends on charge and polarity as well as size.","bullets":["A selectively permeable membrane permits some substances to pass more easily than others.","Small nonpolar molecules can cross the lipid bilayer relatively readily.","Ions and many polar or large molecules require suitable transport proteins or other transport processes.","Pores and channels provide selective pathways through the membrane.","Size alone does not determine whether a molecule can cross."],"questions":[{"suffix":"size-charge","stem":"Why can a small chloride ion not simply cross the lipid core as easily as oxygen?","options":["Chloride is a whole organelle.","Oxygen is always larger than every protein.","Every small molecule passes through equally.","The ion is charged, and the hydrophobic lipid interior is a barrier."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Small size is not enough. Charged ions require suitable transport pathways; small nonpolar oxygen can diffuse through the lipid core."}],"clarification":"The slide says small molecules pass freely. This is too broad: charge, polarity, and lipid solubility matter. The page 34 picture also wrongly depicts Cl- moving by simple diffusion through the lipid bilayer; ions need transport proteins.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Passive Transport","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport"}]},{"key":"diffusion","title":"Diffusion and passive transport","pages":[33],"summary":"Diffusion produces net movement down a concentration gradient without cellular energy input.","bullets":["Molecules are in continual random motion because they possess kinetic energy.","A concentration gradient is a difference in concentration between regions.","Net diffusion is from higher toward lower concentration when a pathway is available.","No extra metabolic energy from the cell is required for passive diffusion.","Individual molecules can move in either direction; the net movement follows the gradient."],"questions":[{"suffix":"direction","stem":"Oxygen concentration is higher outside a cell than inside, and oxygen can cross its membrane. What is the expected net passive movement?","options":["Into the cell, down the concentration gradient.","Out of the cell, against the concentration gradient.","No movement because ATP is absent.","Only toward the cell wall."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With a pathway available, oxygen diffuses from higher to lower concentration without the cell supplying ATP."},{"suffix":"equilibrium","stem":"After concentrations become equal in two connected regions, what happens to molecules?","options":["Every molecule stops moving.","They all move permanently to the larger region.","They continue moving randomly, with no net diffusion between the regions.","They require ATP to move at all."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Equal concentrations mean no net movement caused by a concentration difference. Random molecular movement continues."}],"clarification":"High-to-low describes net movement, not a rule that every individual molecule moves in only one direction.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Passive Transport","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport"}]},{"key":"facilitated-diffusion","title":"Facilitated diffusion","pages":[34],"summary":"Transport proteins can allow passive movement down a gradient.","bullets":["Some substances cross membranes through selective channels or carrier proteins.","This protein-assisted movement is facilitated diffusion when net movement is down the relevant gradient without cellular energy input.","Channels provide pathways; carriers bind material and change conformation during transport.","Using a membrane protein does not by itself mean transport is active."],"questions":[{"suffix":"distinguish","stem":"A substance moves down its gradient through a membrane carrier without the cell supplying energy. Which process is occurring?","options":["Phagocytosis.","Facilitated diffusion.","ATP-driven pumping against a gradient.","Cell division."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Facilitated diffusion uses transport proteins while remaining passive. Protein assistance and energy use are different characteristics."},{"suffix":"chloride","stem":"The source image places Cl- under simple diffusion through the lipid bilayer. Which correction is appropriate?","options":["Chloride is nonpolar and never needs a pathway.","All ions are too large to move across any membrane.","Only chloroplasts can move chloride.","Chloride crosses through suitable transport proteins, not freely through the lipid core."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cl- is charged. The source picture is misleading: an ion moving passively needs a suitable channel or other transporter, and its net passive movement follows the electrochemical gradient."}],"clarification":"The source names channels only, but facilitated diffusion includes carriers. Its Cl- diagram must not be used as evidence that a charged ion freely crosses the lipid bilayer.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Passive Transport","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport"}]},{"key":"diffusion-factors","title":"Factors affecting diffusion","pages":[35],"summary":"Temperature, molecular properties, and the gradient influence diffusion.","bullets":["Higher temperature generally increases molecular motion and diffusion rate when other factors are comparable.","Smaller molecules generally diffuse faster than larger ones in the same medium.","A steeper concentration gradient generally increases net diffusion.","Charge and polarity affect passage through a lipid membrane.","Compare one variable at a time when interpreting a diffusion experiment."],"questions":[{"suffix":"gradient","stem":"Two otherwise identical diffusion setups differ only in concentration difference: 2 units versus 8 units. Which should initially show faster net diffusion?","options":["The 2-unit difference, because a gradient opposes diffusion.","Both must have zero diffusion.","The 8-unit difference, because the gradient is steeper.","Neither can diffuse without ATP."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A larger concentration difference gives a stronger gradient and generally a faster initial net diffusion rate."},{"suffix":"controlled","stem":"Which comparison isolates the effect of temperature on diffusion?","options":["Same molecule, medium, and gradient; different temperatures.","Different molecules, temperatures, and gradients at once.","Same temperature but different molecule sizes.","Different membranes and different solutes with no other controls."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing temperature while keeping other important factors comparable lets you interpret a difference in diffusion rate."}],"clarification":"The slide says neutral molecules freely cross membranes. Neutrality alone is insufficient: large polar molecules can still face a barrier. Size, polarity, charge, and the pathway all matter.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Passive Transport","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport"}]},{"key":"osmosis","title":"Osmosis","pages":[36],"summary":"Osmosis is net water movement across a selectively permeable membrane.","bullets":["Water can move across a selectively permeable membrane.","For the introductory comparison, assume the membrane allows water but prevents the compared solute from crossing.","Net water movement is toward the side with higher concentration of the nonpenetrating solute.","Water molecules move in both directions; osmosis describes the net movement."],"questions":[{"suffix":"net-water","stem":"A membrane permits water but not a solute. Side A has 2% solute and side B has 6%. What is the initial net movement of water?","options":["From B to A.","No water can move through a selective membrane.","Water turns into solute on side A.","From A to B."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Under these assumptions, net water movement is toward the more concentrated nonpenetrating-solute solution, from A to B."},{"suffix":"substance","stem":"Which moving substance defines osmosis?","options":["A protein inside a vesicle.","Water crossing a selectively permeable membrane.","DNA passing through a ribosome.","A bacterium entering a food vacuole."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Osmosis specifically concerns water movement across a selectively permeable membrane, rather than every type of diffusion."}],"clarification":"The source says water moves in an attempt to equilibrate. Water has no intention; random motion and differences in water potential produce net movement. The simple solute comparison assumes similar pressures and a nonpenetrating solute.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Passive Transport","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport"}]},{"key":"tonicity","title":"Isotonic, hypertonic, and hypotonic solutions","pages":[36],"summary":"Tonicity predicts a cell's water gain or loss relative to its surroundings.","bullets":["Isotonic surroundings cause no net osmotic change in cell water content; water still moves in both directions.","Hypertonic surroundings have a higher effective concentration of nonpenetrating solute than the cell and draw water out.","Hypotonic surroundings have a lower effective concentration of nonpenetrating solute and drive water into the cell.","Animal cells can shrink after losing water or swell after gaining water.","The source also shows plant cells: the wall affects shape changes as water is gained or lost.","In a hypertonic solution, a plant cell loses water; its vacuole and cell contents shrink and the plasma membrane can pull away from the wall (plasmolysis). In a hypotonic solution, water enters and the central vacuole expands; the wall limits expansion and the cell becomes turgid."],"questions":[{"suffix":"hypertonic","stem":"An animal cell is placed in a hypertonic solution. What initial change is expected?","options":["Water leaves and the cell shrinks.","Water enters and the cell swells.","ATP must force all water out.","The cell immediately develops a cellulose wall."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A hypertonic environment draws net water out of the cell, so its volume decreases."},{"suffix":"three-cases","stem":"Which set correctly matches the surrounding solution with the usual net water response?","options":["Isotonic: water stops; hypertonic: water enters; hypotonic: water leaves.","Isotonic: water leaves; hypertonic: no net movement; hypotonic: water stops.","Isotonic: no net water movement; hypertonic: water leaves; hypotonic: water enters.","All three cause the same net water movement."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tonicity is relative to the cell. Isotonic means balanced net water movement, hypertonic causes net water loss, and hypotonic causes net water gain."},{"suffix":"plant-cell","stem":"A plant cell is moved into a hypertonic solution. Which change matches the plant-cell diagram in the source?","options":["Water enters and the vacuole expands until the cell wall dissolves.","Water leaves, and the vacuole and cell contents shrink so the membrane may pull away from the wall.","The cell wall contracts and actively pumps water into the vacuole.","Water stops moving because the plant has a cell wall."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A hypertonic outside solution draws net water out of the cell. The vacuole and protoplast shrink while the wall stays relatively rigid, so the membrane can pull away from the wall. This is plasmolysis."}],"clarification":"Interpret concentration as effective nonpenetrating-solute concentration. Isotonic does not mean water molecules stop. Plant cell walls constrain swelling, so plant and animal cells need not show identical shape changes.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Passive Transport","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport"}]},{"key":"active-transport","title":"Active transport and cellular energy","pages":[37],"summary":"Cells use energy to support transport that passive movement cannot accomplish.","bullets":["Moving a solute against its concentration gradient means moving it from lower toward higher concentration.","The source identifies ATP, adenosine triphosphate, as an important cellular energy source.","The lecture also groups energy-requiring transport of large material with active transport.","Distinguish solute pumping through transport proteins from the vesicular bulk transport shown next.","A transport process is not active merely because it uses a protein."],"questions":[{"suffix":"against-gradient","stem":"A cell moves an uncharged solute from a lower concentration inside to a higher concentration outside. Which feature requires an energy source?","options":["The solute is moving down its concentration gradient.","The solute is moving against its concentration gradient.","Any movement toward the outside is always passive.","The cell has reached equal concentrations."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Moving this uncharged solute against its concentration gradient requires coupling to an energy source, unlike passive movement down the gradient."},{"suffix":"compare","stem":"Which observation most clearly identifies active rather than passive solute transport?","options":["Molecules are moving.","A cell membrane is present.","A transport protein is involved.","Energy is used to move a solute against its gradient."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Protein involvement alone does not distinguish active transport from facilitated diffusion. Energy-coupled movement against a gradient does."}],"clarification":"ATP directly powers primary active transport. Secondary active transport uses stored gradient energy indirectly supplied by metabolism. Large cargo generally uses vesicular transport rather than crossing through a pump as an intact large package.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Active Transport","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-3-active-transport"}]},{"key":"phagocytosis","title":"Phagocytosis","pages":[38],"summary":"Phagocytosis brings large particles into a cell by engulfing them.","bullets":["The source uses the memory phrase cell eating.","The plasma membrane surrounds a large particle.","A membrane-enclosed compartment forms around the engulfed material.","Phagocytosis is a type of endocytosis and requires cellular energy."],"questions":[{"suffix":"particle","stem":"A cell surrounds a bacterium and encloses it in an internal compartment. Which process fits?","options":["Osmosis.","Simple diffusion.","Phagocytosis.","Exocytosis."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Phagocytosis is the engulfment of large particles. The source illustrates the membrane surrounding cargo to bring it inside."}]},{"key":"pinocytosis","title":"Pinocytosis","pages":[38],"summary":"Pinocytosis takes in extracellular fluid in small vesicles.","bullets":["The source uses the memory phrase cell drinking.","Small amounts of extracellular fluid and dissolved substances enter in membrane vesicles.","Pinocytosis is a type of endocytosis.","It is different from water molecules individually crossing a membrane during osmosis."],"questions":[{"suffix":"fluid","stem":"A cell takes in droplets of surrounding fluid in small membrane vesicles. What is this?","options":["Pinocytosis.","Osmosis.","A protein pump moving one ion.","Facilitated diffusion."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pinocytosis brings fluid into the cell in vesicles. Osmosis describes water moving across a membrane, not the formation of fluid-filled vesicles."}]},{"key":"exocytosis","title":"Exocytosis and secretion","pages":[38],"summary":"Exocytosis releases vesicle contents outside a cell.","bullets":["The source describes exocytosis as the outward counterpart to phagocytosis and pinocytosis.","Material produced or packaged inside the cell is carried toward the surface in a vesicle.","The vesicle membrane fuses with the plasma membrane, releasing contents to the outside.","Secretion is an important use of this pathway."],"questions":[{"suffix":"release","stem":"A vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane and releases a protein outside the cell. Which process occurs?","options":["Phagocytosis.","Isotonic osmosis.","Cytoplasmic streaming only.","Exocytosis."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Exocytosis moves packaged cargo out of a cell by membrane fusion, rather than by pushing a large protein directly through the lipid core."}]},{"key":"protein-pumps","title":"Protein pumps","pages":[38],"summary":"Energy-coupled transport proteins move selected substances across membranes.","bullets":["The source places specialized protein pumps within the membrane.","These pumps move selected substances across the membrane.","The ATP-powered examples use chemical energy to drive transport.","Pumps differ from passive channels even though both are membrane proteins."],"questions":[{"suffix":"pump-channel","stem":"Which description distinguishes an ATP-powered pump from a passive channel?","options":["A pump is made of cellulose.","A pump uses energy to drive transport; a passive channel allows movement down a gradient.","A passive channel creates a vesicle around each ion.","A pump can function only outside a cell."],"answer":1,"explanation":"An ATP-powered pump couples energy to transport, whereas a passive channel permits movement down the relevant gradient."},{"suffix":"inhibition","stem":"An ATP-powered pump loses access to ATP while the membrane remains intact. What is the most direct expected effect?","options":["All random molecular movement stops.","Every molecule immediately crosses the lipid core.","Transport driven by that pump decreases or stops.","Phospholipid heads reverse their orientation."],"answer":2,"explanation":"That pump depends on ATP for its transport cycle. Loss of ATP does not make all passive molecular movement stop."}]},{"key":"membrane-trafficking","title":"Organelle membranes and vesicle traffic","pages":[39],"summary":"Budding and fusion connect compartments of the endomembrane system.","bullets":["Organelle membranes share a lipid-bilayer organization with the plasma membrane.","Vesicles can bud from one membrane and fuse with another.","The source example follows membrane and cargo from ER toward the Golgi.","Exocytosis can add vesicle membrane to the plasma membrane; endocytosis removes membrane into the cell.","The membrane route is a trafficking relationship, not proof that all cellular membranes are identical."],"questions":[{"suffix":"route","stem":"Which route correctly describes the source's ER-to-Golgi membrane traffic?","options":["The ER becomes a chromosome and enters the Golgi nucleus.","A ribosome dissolves the entire cell membrane.","A chloroplast is pushed through a protein channel.","A transport vesicle buds from ER and fuses with a Golgi membrane."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Membrane budding and fusion allow cargo and membrane components to move through the endomembrane system."}],"clarification":"The slide calls organelle membranes identical to the plasma membrane and mentions cholesterol everywhere. Membranes share a general bilayer plan but differ in proteins and lipids. Its ER-to-Golgi carrier is better described as a transport vesicle than a large vacuole.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Endomembrane System","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins"},{"title":"OpenStax: Membrane Components and Structure","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-1-components-and-structure"}]},{"key":"surface-area-volume","title":"Calculating surface area and volume","pages":[40,41,42],"summary":"For similarly shaped cells, volume increases faster than surface area as size increases.","bullets":["Membrane surface area represents the available boundary for exchange.","Cell volume represents the amount of internal material with nutritional and waste-removal demands.","For a cube with side length L, surface area SA = 6L squared and volume V = L cubed.","For side lengths 1, 2, and 3 units, the source gives surface areas 6, 24, and 54 square units.","The corresponding volumes are 1, 8, and 27 cubic units.","The SA:V ratios are 6:1, 3:1, and 2:1.","Doubling side length multiplies area by 4 and volume by 8; tripling multiplies area by 9 and volume by 27."],"questions":[{"suffix":"cube","stem":"A cube-shaped cell has side length 2 units. What are its surface area and volume?","options":["SA = 24 square units; V = 8 cubic units.","SA = 8 square units; V = 24 cubic units.","SA = 12 square units; V = 4 cubic units.","SA = 6 square units; V = 2 cubic units."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For L = 2, SA = 6 x 2 squared = 24 and V = 2 cubed = 8. The surface-area-to-volume ratio is 3:1."},{"suffix":"scaling","stem":"A cube's side length increases from 1 to 3 units. How do area and volume change?","options":["Area increases 27-fold; volume increases 9-fold.","Area and volume both increase 3-fold.","Area increases 9-fold; volume increases 27-fold.","Area stays constant; volume increases 3-fold."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Area scales with the square of side length and volume with the cube: 3 squared = 9 and 3 cubed = 27."}]},{"key":"cell-size-limits","title":"Surface area, volume, and cell-size limits","pages":[43],"summary":"A falling surface-area-to-volume ratio makes exchange harder to meet relative to demand.","bullets":["As a similarly shaped cell grows, its volume increases faster than its membrane area.","There is less exchange surface for each unit of cell volume.","Obtaining nutrients and removing wastes can become limiting.","Division into smaller cells can increase total surface area relative to the same total volume.","The source uses this comparison to explain one pressure limiting cell size."],"questions":[{"suffix":"exchange","stem":"Why can growth create an exchange problem for a cube-shaped cell?","options":["The nucleus always disappears when a cell grows.","Volume grows faster than area, leaving less exchange surface per unit volume.","Surface area falls to zero as side length increases.","Larger cells have no membranes."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cell has more internal volume to support relative to its exchange area. This can make transport insufficient for its needs."},{"suffix":"division","stem":"A cube of side 2 is divided into eight cubes of side 1. What happens to total surface area and total volume?","options":["Both decrease by half.","Area stays at 24 and volume rises to 16.","Volume disappears because division consumes it.","Total area rises from 24 to 48 while total volume stays 8."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Eight small cubes have total area 8 x 6 = 48 and volume 8 x 1 = 8. Division increases exposed surface relative to the same total volume."}],"clarification":"The source ends with divide or die. Treat this as a simplified illustration of growth limits, not a universal outcome: cells can stop growing or use shapes and structures that improve exchange.","sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: Prokaryotic Cells","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells"},{"title":"OpenStax: Passive Transport","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport"}]}],"sourcePages":[{"page":1,"text":"CELL \nBIOLOGY"},{"page":2,"text":"Core Concepts:\n• Cell Theory\n• What Is a Cell\n• Pro & Eukaryotic Cells\n• Organelles\n• Cell Membranes\n• Transport in & out of cells"},{"page":3,"text":"Around 1590 AD, \nEuropean scientists \nbegan focusing their \ncrude microscopes on \na variety of samples, \nincluding pond water \n& tree bark.\nCell Theory"},{"page":4,"text":"Antonie Van \nLeeuwenhoek\n• 1632 - 1723\n• self taught amateur \nnaturalist\n• often credited as creating \nthe first microscope\n• first to observe bacteria & \nprotists\nCell Theory"},{"page":5,"text":"Robert Hooke\n• 1635 - 1703\n• published ‘Micrographia’ \nin 1665\n• examined thin slices of \ncork \n• saw tiny box-like \ncompartments, which he \ntermed “cellulae”\nCell Theory"},{"page":6,"text":"Mattias Schleiden \n• 1804 - 1881\n• Lawyer & botanist\n• concluded that all \nplants are composed \nof cells.\nCell Theory"},{"page":7,"text":"Theodor Schwann \n• 1810 - 1882\n• physician & physiologist\n• concluded that all animal \ntissues are composed of \ncells.\nCell Theory"},{"page":8,"text":"Rudolf Virchow\n• 1821 - 1902\n• proposed that cells \ncan only arise from \npre-existing cells\nCell Theory"},{"page":9,"text":"Cell Theory has 3 tenets:\nAll living organisms consist of one or more \ncells\nThe cell is the basic unit of structure for all \norganisms\nAll cells only arise from pre-existing cells\n"},{"page":10,"text":"• A cell is the simplest unit of \nliving matter.\n• There are 2 general categories \nof cells:\n⚬ prokaryotic\n⚬ eukaryotic"},{"page":11,"text":"Prokaryotic cells\n• pro = early, primitive, before\n• kary = genes, refers to DNA / \nRNA (ie. nucleus)\n• These are simple, small cells \n• they lack a nucleus and \nmembrane-bound organelles.  \n"},{"page":12,"text":"Prokaryotic cells\n• Bacteria & cyanobacteria \n(unicellular algae) are examples \n• These cells have DNA & RNA \nloose in the cytoplasm.\n• 3 common shapes are cocci \n(round), bacilli (rod), spirillum \n(wavy or spiral)\n "},{"page":13,"text":"Prokaryotic cells"},{"page":14,"text":"Eukaryotic cells\n• eu = true, real\n• Multicellular algae, protozoans, \nfungi, plants & animals are all \ncomposed of eukaryotic cells. \n"},{"page":15,"text":"Eukaryotic cells\n• These cells are significantly larger & \nstructurally more complex than \nprokaryotes.  \n• They all contain membrane-bound \norganelles (specialized structures that \nperform specific functions)\n"},{"page":16,"text":"Plant Cell\n• cell wall\n• cell membrane\n• cytoplasm\n• chloroplast\n• endoplasmic reticulum\n• golgi body\n• mitochondria\n• nucleus\n• nucleolus\n• vacuole\n"},{"page":17,"text":"Animal Cell\n• cell membrane\n• cytoplasm\n• golgi body\n• nucleus\n• mitochondria\n• smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n• rough endoplasmic reticulum\n• vacuole\n"},{"page":18,"text":"Flagella & Cilia\n• Usually one (flagellum), \n• or a few (flagella), \n• long projections, used for \nlocomotion\n• are external projections \nused for cell locomotion,\n• or for moving substances \nalong the surface of a cell.  \n• They are made of tubules of \nprotein. \n• Usually numerous cilia\n• short projections\n• cover all or a portion of the cell"},{"page":19,"text":"Cell Wall\n• provides support & protection to \nthe cell.\n• Algae & plants -   cell wall is made \nof cellulose (β glucose chains)\n• Fungi – cell wall is made of chitin"},{"page":20,"text":"Cell Membrane\n• selectively allows materials to pass in & out of the cell.  \n• is the outer living boundary of the cell.\n• is made of phospholipids & proteins\n"},{"page":21,"text":"cytoplasm\n• cyto =  cell, plasm = goop\n• Cytoplasm includes everything inside the cell \nmembrane & outside the nucleus.  \n• made up of fluid (H2O) & the cytoskeleton\n• provides shape & support to the cell.  \n• Movement of the fluid, called cytoplasmic \nstreaming, helps disperse nutrients \nthroughout the cell. \nCytoplasm"},{"page":22,"text":"Nucleus\n• usually spherical or oval,\n• often the largest structure inside a cell\n• contains the cell’s hereditary information \n(DNA),\n• separated from the cytoplasm by a double \nmembrane.  \n• fluid inside the nucleus is called \nnucleoplasm.  \n• also contains nucleoli, the site of ribosome \nproduction, \n• and DNA & proteins in the form of \nchromatin or chromosomes.\nNucleus"},{"page":23,"text":"smooth ER\nrough ER\nEndoplasmic Reticulum\n• A system of pairs of parallel membranes that enclose \nnarrow cavities, the ER forms a network of canals.  \n• It is continuous with the cell membrane and the \nnuclear membrane.  \n• Lipids (fats) are made here, and proteins (made at \nribosomes) are transported here.  \n• The ER provides a surface area for chemical \nreactions, a pathway for the transport of molecules, \nand a storage area for synthesized molecules.\n• There are 2 types of ER: \n⚬ Smooth (SER) lacks ribosomes\n⚬ Rough (RER) lined with ribosomes"},{"page":24,"text":"• are NOT membrane bound. \n• These small, dense, spherical bodies are made of \nstrands of rRNA (ribosomal RNA).  \n• Ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis \n• They are found free in the cytoplasm, attached to \nRER, or as polysomes.\n \nA polysome = a cluster of ribosomes.  This provides a \nsite for the production of proteins needed within a \ncell.\n \nRibosomes\nribosomes"},{"page":25,"text":"Golgi\n• Golgi consist of 4-8 flattened disks, \ncalled  ‘cisternae’, \n• which bulge at the ends to bud off \nspherical vesicles.\n• Golgi may sometimes connect to ER, \n• it’s function is to package & secrete \nproteins, lipids, & carbohydrates.\nGolgi body"},{"page":26,"text":"• These small organelles are bean-shaped,                                         filamentous & \nappear throughout the cytoplasm. \n• Mitochondria have a double membrane system                                  enclosing a \nfluid-filled matrix.  \n• The outer membrane is smooth, while the inner is                                      highly \nconvoluted.\n• This provides a large surface area for the reactions of cellular respiration:\nThe mitochondria produced adenosine triphosphate (ATP), which is the energy \ncurrency of cells.\nMitochondria"},{"page":27,"text":"• all are membrane enclosed spheres \nused to store or transport materials in \ncells.\n• Vacuoles – large compartments\n• Vesicles – tiny compartments  \n• Lysosomes – specialized compartments \nthat are filled with digestive \n(proteolytic) enzymes.\nvesicles & vacuoles"},{"page":28,"text":"chloroplasts\n• Chloroplasts are unique to algae & \nplant cells.\n• These membranous structures contain \nchlorophyll & the enzymes needed for \nphotosynthesis in stacks called \n‘thylakoids’. \nchloroplasts\nouter membrane\ninner membrane thylakoid"},{"page":29,"text":"Membrane Structure:\nThe cell membrane is made of a double layer of phospholipid \nmolecules with proteins scattered throughout.\n"},{"page":30,"text":"Membrane Structure:\n• Phospholipids have a hydrophilic phosphate end, and a hydrophobic lipid \nend.\n⚬ hydro= water      philia = loving      phobia = fearing\n• When placed in water, the lipids align together in the middle, sandwiched by \nthe phosphates, to form a bilayer.  \n• There are also proteins & cholesterol found within the membrane."},{"page":31,"text":"The membrane is described as a Fluid Mosaic model, because:\n• Fluid - the component parts are able to move or flow laterally\n• Mosaic - the membrane is made of different parts, like a mosaic\nProtein\nCholesterol\nPhospholipids"},{"page":32,"text":"Membranes are semi-permeable\n• they permit some molecules to pass through, but \nprevent or slow others. \n• Small Molecules: can pass through freely   \n• Large Molecules: generally prevented from \npassing.  \n• Some may move through pores or channels."},{"page":33,"text":"Passive \nTransport\n• most small molecules cross the membrane by \ndiffusion.  \n• They will naturally flow from an area of high \nconcentration to one of low concentration. \n• due to the inherent energy possessed by all molecules \n• No additional energy is required for diffusion to occur\nhigh\nconcentration\nlow\nconcentration"},{"page":34,"text":"Passive \nTransport\n• Some molecules diffuse only though specific \nmembrane protein channels.  \n• This is called facilitated diffusion.\nsimple diffusion\nfacilitated \ntransport"},{"page":35,"text":"Factors affecting diffusion rate:\n• Temperature - molecules move faster at higher temperatures, \nso diffusion would accelerate.\n• Size - smaller molecules diffuse faster than larger ones.\n• Charge - neutral molecules freely pass through cell \nmembranes, but charged molecules are often blocked.\n• Concentration gradient – the higher the concentration \ndifference, the faster diffusion occurs."},{"page":36,"text":"• The movement of water across membranes by \ndiffusion is called osmosis\n• Water will move in an attempt to equilibrate.\n• Isotonic  = the same concentration inside the cell \nas outside.  Molecules enter & leave the cell at the \nsame rate.\n• Hypertonic = more concentrated outside. Water \nleaves the cell & it shrivels\n• Hypotonic = less concentrated outside.  Water \nenters the cell & it swells\nOsmosis\n"},{"page":37,"text":"• to move molecules against a concentration gradient (from an \narea of lower concentration to one of higher concentration)\n• to move large molecules across the membrane. (proteins, \nhormones, enzymes)\n• Cellular energy, in the form of  ATP is used.   \n• ATP =  Adenosine Tri Phosphate\nActive transport uses energy"},{"page":38,"text":"Forms of \nActive \nTransport\n• Phagocytosis = cell eating.  This is when the cell \nengulfs a large particle, creating a vacuole.\n• Pinocytosis = cell drinking. This is when small droplets move into \nthe cell, creating vesicles\n• Exocytosis/secretion= opposite of phago/pinocytosis.  Material produced \nin the cell is moved to the outside.\n• protein pumps = specialized protein molecules embedded in the \nmembrane move materials across, using energy (ATP) in the process."},{"page":39,"text":"• Membranes surrounding organelles is identical to the cell \nmembrane.  \n• It is composed of a phospholipid bilayer, with proteins & cholesterol \nembedded within.\n• Due to endo & exocytosis, segments of membranes within cells can \ninterchange: \n• Membrane that begins as endoplasmic reticulum can pinch off as a \nvacuole and then migrate & fuse to the golgi.\norganelle membranes"},{"page":40,"text":"• The cell membrane determines how much material can enter and leave a \ncell.   \n• The size of the membrane (surface area) determines the rate of transport of \nmaterials. \n• The cytoplasm determines how much food the cell needs & how much waste \nit produces (volume).\n• Mathematically, these two factors do not increase at the same rate. \nsurface area: volume ratio"},{"page":41,"text":"• If  we assume that a cell is a cube, we can compare the surface area & \nvolume as it grows:\nsurface area: volume ratio\n1\n2\n3\n• Surface Area = length x width x 6\n• Volume = length x width x height\nSA = 6\nSA = 24\nSA = 54V = 1\nV = 8\nV = 27"},{"page":42,"text":"• As the cell grows, its volume increases at a faster rate than its surface area. \nsurface area: volume ratio\nSA = 6 SA = 24 SA = 54\nV = 1 V = 8 V = 27\nx 4\nx 9\nx 8\nx 27"},{"page":43,"text":"• As the cell grows, its volume increases at a faster rate than its \nsurface area. \n• This means that the volume (amount of cytoplasm) outgrows the \nsurface area’s (cell membrane’s) ability to bring in nutrients & \nremove wastes to keep the cell alive.  \n• At this point, the cell will either need to divide, or it will die.\nsurface area: volume ratio"}]};
